1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:04,760
In Colorado, a father and son struggle.

2
00:00:05,280 --> 00:00:07,460
Hey! Move that mean back!

3
00:00:07,840 --> 00:00:10,800
To move this fragile homestead across a mountain.

4
00:00:12,900 --> 00:00:14,300
Ho, ho, ho, ho!

5
00:00:14,720 --> 00:00:22,200
And in Illinois, three generations of movers shoulder the hopes of an entire town to save a Victorian masterpiece.

6
00:00:23,280 --> 00:00:28,400
Facing overwhelming odds, these crews risk everything to get the job done.

7
00:00:28,401 --> 00:00:29,960
Mega Movers.

8
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:32,160
Next on Modern Marvels.

9
00:00:42,920 --> 00:00:44,760
Aspen Park, Colorado.

10
00:00:46,140 --> 00:00:48,880
8,500 feet up in the Rocky Mountains.

11
00:00:49,580 --> 00:00:56,540
This 1870s homestead with a log cabin and barn are endangered remnants of a bygone era.

12
00:00:57,100 --> 00:01:03,180
This part of Colorado has a very rich history going back thousands of years to the original Indian inhabitants

13
00:01:03,181 --> 00:01:06,780
and then to the early settlers and homesteaders and miners who lived here.

14
00:01:07,480 --> 00:01:11,540
The homestead's owner, Norm Meyer, has sold this piece of his property,

15
00:01:11,840 --> 00:01:14,820
but wants the buildings moved to save them from demolition.

16
00:01:14,940 --> 00:01:22,540
The cabin is 135 years old or so. It was built in 1870.

17
00:01:22,541 --> 00:01:25,660
The barn was built around 1910 or so.

18
00:01:26,040 --> 00:01:28,600
The first owner of the cabin was John Lubin.

19
00:01:29,060 --> 00:01:34,260
John Lubin was a farmer and rancher who built his homestead along the original stagecoach route

20
00:01:34,261 --> 00:01:37,080
between Denver and the surrounding mining communities.

21
00:01:37,420 --> 00:01:41,300
John Lubin was described as a disagreeable person by his neighbors.

22
00:01:42,100 --> 00:01:45,400
Several members of the Lubin family died mysterious deaths.

23
00:01:46,340 --> 00:01:51,000
Those mysterious deaths have fed rumors that the buildings are haunted.

24
00:01:51,580 --> 00:01:52,640
It ain't sturdy.

25
00:01:53,060 --> 00:01:55,900
Well, of course it's not sturdy. That's why they called us to fix this.

26
00:01:56,900 --> 00:02:00,820
Rocky Mountain Structural Movers has been called in to get the job done.

27
00:02:01,360 --> 00:02:02,840
Its owner is Bill Davis.

28
00:02:03,540 --> 00:02:04,700
This is going to be tough.

29
00:02:05,160 --> 00:02:07,860
One thing we don't want this thing to do is once we start lifting,

30
00:02:07,980 --> 00:02:10,500
we don't want this wall to decide to just come on out on us.

31
00:02:10,800 --> 00:02:13,980
Bill has been moving structures for over 30 years,

32
00:02:14,180 --> 00:02:18,740
including a 100-year-old train depot and a 1,500-ton bridge.

33
00:02:19,040 --> 00:02:20,720
I'm a third-generation house mover.

34
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:23,260
which makes my son a fourth-generation house mover.

35
00:02:23,780 --> 00:02:28,480
He's pretty young right now, and he's going to college to get his structural engineering degree,

36
00:02:28,640 --> 00:02:29,640
and then we'll take it from there.

37
00:02:29,800 --> 00:02:33,580
We have our ups and downs, you know, because he has an idea about something,

38
00:02:33,660 --> 00:02:35,800
and I have an idea about it, and they kind of collide.

39
00:02:36,240 --> 00:02:38,960
So when we come in here with our cross beams,

40
00:02:39,340 --> 00:02:43,240
we'll do something in here as an added support that this building can't rack.

41
00:02:43,241 --> 00:02:45,660
I don't see any reason to go down the middle with it.

42
00:02:45,661 --> 00:02:58,040
The buildings will be moved to the other side of Norm's 130-acre property to be near his 1889 Victorian house, a former Stagecoats Depot and historical landmark.

43
00:02:58,960 --> 00:03:05,500
To do this, Bill is faced with the unique challenge of building a road across two miles of mountainside.

44
00:03:06,060 --> 00:03:08,567
We're looking for ruts, we're looking for rocks.

45
00:03:08,568 --> 00:03:12,640
We just want a smooth ride, especially with these fragile log homes like this.

46
00:03:13,540 --> 00:03:18,100
Contractor Brad Cranebring will use a bulldozer to cut a swath through several obstacles.

47
00:03:18,760 --> 00:03:21,160
First, they will have to navigate this steep descent.

48
00:03:21,820 --> 00:03:24,320
All right, well, here's our ascent, and I don't know, Brad, what do you think?

49
00:03:24,400 --> 00:03:27,060
This one could be a little difficult in through here.

50
00:03:27,740 --> 00:03:33,420
As soon as we come down the hill, we'll make our turn, come around, and then start heading down this way here.

51
00:03:34,060 --> 00:03:39,300
Next, Bill will have to maneuver the barn underneath these dangerous high-voltage power lines

52
00:03:39,301 --> 00:03:44,380
and finally negotiate this sharp 90-degree turn onto a narrow road.

53
00:03:45,020 --> 00:03:46,360
Boy, I hope that's wide enough.

54
00:03:47,680 --> 00:03:50,560
We can get 32 foot through here if we grease the building.

55
00:04:04,180 --> 00:04:06,900
Two days later, the dirt road is complete.

56
00:04:06,901 --> 00:04:10,718
We're getting ready to start on the barn. Everything's here.

57
00:04:10,719 --> 00:04:13,200
We're ready to rock and roll this baby.

58
00:04:14,520 --> 00:04:18,940
It's time for Bill to build the iron framework to support the barn from underneath.

59
00:04:19,700 --> 00:04:23,420
Four 8-inch crossbeams are inserted to support the outer walls,

60
00:04:23,720 --> 00:04:28,700
and two 20-inch main beams will support the entire structure and be attached to the wheels.

61
00:04:29,640 --> 00:04:32,360
The process is known as stabbing iron.

62
00:04:36,900 --> 00:04:50,900
Push down on it.

63
00:04:51,040 --> 00:04:53,280
Okay, push.

64
00:04:55,420 --> 00:04:56,820
Right in.

65
00:05:06,900 --> 00:05:15,300
All right, I want you to go around James and look for weak points around this barn and

66
00:05:15,301 --> 00:05:19,540
start considering where you want to sandwich, okay?

67
00:05:19,541 --> 00:05:23,860
These hand-hewn timbers of Douglas fir and yellow pine were joined with saddle-notch

68
00:05:23,861 --> 00:05:29,100
construction in 1910, a popular technique among early settlers.

69
00:05:29,101 --> 00:05:33,700
But 95 years of inclement weather has taken its toll.

70
00:05:33,701 --> 00:05:36,620
This is a failure point right through here.

71
00:05:36,621 --> 00:05:41,940
So what we're going to do is we're going to sandwich these logs to the inside wall.

72
00:05:41,941 --> 00:05:46,960
If we pick it up right now, if there's any rot all the way through, that they'll just

73
00:05:46,961 --> 00:05:50,980
snap in half and it could collapse the wall.

74
00:05:50,981 --> 00:05:56,340
James braces the wall by bolting a piece of plywood on the outside to a piece on the

75
00:05:56,341 --> 00:06:00,160
inside, creating a makeshift vise.

76
00:06:00,161 --> 00:06:03,180
He then takes wood shims to fill in the gaps.

77
00:06:03,181 --> 00:06:05,940
We don't want the logs to be able to close together.

78
00:06:05,941 --> 00:06:08,840
Whatever the gap is, we want to maintain that gap.

79
00:06:08,841 --> 00:06:13,740
So we just take different sizes of wood, just enough to wedge it in, pound it in, and that's

80
00:06:13,741 --> 00:06:15,060
good enough.

81
00:06:15,061 --> 00:06:21,580
Twenty-five and fifty-ton hydraulic jacks are set under the crossbeams in order to finally

82
00:06:21,581 --> 00:06:23,500
lift the front of the barn.

83
00:06:23,501 --> 00:06:26,000
They call the procedure the pick.

84
00:06:26,001 --> 00:06:30,500
It is the first time these logs will move in almost 100 years.

85
00:06:30,501 --> 00:06:37,440
You know, just being able to get underneath the logs and lift the building without any failures is a challenge in itself.

86
00:06:37,620 --> 00:06:39,240
There's a lot of variables that come into play.

87
00:06:39,480 --> 00:06:42,340
If you don't take your time, take it easy. You can really mess something up.

88
00:06:43,080 --> 00:06:44,300
Doesn't get much prettier than that.

89
00:06:44,900 --> 00:06:49,500
These jacks are attached to the central pump, and Bill is ready for liftoff.

90
00:06:50,000 --> 00:06:52,280
Okay, men. We're ready for our pick?

91
00:06:52,960 --> 00:06:54,460
All right, here goes the first pick.

92
00:07:00,500 --> 00:07:10,500
We have lift.

93
00:07:17,500 --> 00:07:26,120
It starts with one inch, and it goes to two, ten, twelve, two feet, and that's the beginning of our lift.

94
00:07:26,121 --> 00:07:30,940
We just don't know what it's going to do yet.

95
00:07:30,980 --> 00:07:32,000
And it's not over yet.

96
00:07:32,100 --> 00:07:33,400
We still have the other side to lift.

97
00:07:33,640 --> 00:07:35,020
We don't know where the weak link is.

98
00:07:35,360 --> 00:07:37,940
There's got to be one somewhere, and we're trying to prepare for it.

99
00:07:38,940 --> 00:07:41,340
Next, it's time to lift the rear of the barn.

100
00:07:49,620 --> 00:07:51,040
Let's keep it shimmed off.

101
00:07:51,041 --> 00:07:56,240
Come on, you guys. Get some shim moving here.

102
00:07:58,120 --> 00:07:59,980
Let's get that shim in underneath there.

103
00:08:01,280 --> 00:08:02,760
You guys ready to go up a little more?

104
00:08:03,960 --> 00:08:04,900
Still feeling brave?

105
00:08:12,400 --> 00:08:13,920
Oh, I think it's awesome.

106
00:08:14,760 --> 00:08:18,400
I'm amazed that it's going up in one piece yet.

107
00:08:19,680 --> 00:08:20,380
Fantastic.

108
00:08:21,040 --> 00:08:25,040
We've got significant lift here in control of the building, which is what we really want to see.

109
00:08:25,320 --> 00:08:26,900
And I think we're just going to be fine here.

110
00:08:30,920 --> 00:08:34,600
Next on Mega Movers, crisis hits in Colorado.

111
00:08:35,060 --> 00:08:35,760
Hey!

112
00:08:36,040 --> 00:08:36,880
F***ing damn it!

113
00:08:37,020 --> 00:08:38,540
Move that f***ing beam back!

114
00:08:39,160 --> 00:08:43,320
A 120-year-old building is on the verge of tumbling to the ground.

115
00:08:43,321 --> 00:08:44,620
Ho, ho, ho, ho!

116
00:08:44,621 --> 00:08:53,380
The Homestead Act of 1862 granted ownership of 160 acres of public land

117
00:08:53,381 --> 00:08:57,580
to anyone who had lived on and cultivated the land for at least five years.

118
00:08:58,200 --> 00:09:01,920
By 1900, settlers have claimed more than 80 million acres.

119
00:09:02,560 --> 00:09:04,400
Mega Movers will return in a moment.

120
00:09:07,820 --> 00:09:11,260
Well, the Lubin family was living here in 1881,

121
00:09:11,600 --> 00:09:13,700
trying to scratch out a living up here in the mountains,

122
00:09:13,701 --> 00:09:17,020
which was somewhat difficult because of the climate and the isolation.

123
00:09:17,460 --> 00:09:22,640
Six people cooped up in a little cabin all winter with no entertainment

124
00:09:22,641 --> 00:09:25,220
and relatively isolated off by themselves.

125
00:09:25,860 --> 00:09:31,500
No one knows if it was cabin fever, but four members of the Lubin family died mysteriously.

126
00:09:34,520 --> 00:09:37,600
Their spirits may still haunt this homestead.

127
00:09:38,600 --> 00:09:43,300
The crew of Rocky Mountain Structural Movers hopes they're not haunting the move.

128
00:09:43,700 --> 00:09:49,300
The crew's first task is to place the large 22-inch main beams underneath the barn.

129
00:09:49,960 --> 00:09:52,680
These beams weigh 7,000 pounds apiece,

130
00:09:52,860 --> 00:09:56,800
and one wrong move when manipulating this extreme weight could be deadly.

131
00:09:57,640 --> 00:09:58,340
Shane!

132
00:09:58,341 --> 00:09:59,041
What?

133
00:09:59,042 --> 00:09:59,900
Bring that in back.

134
00:10:00,600 --> 00:10:02,400
The level intensity is up right now.

135
00:10:02,401 --> 00:10:05,300
We've kicked it up a notch, and we're getting ready to load this thing on wheels,

136
00:10:05,420 --> 00:10:08,140
and the intensity will stay like this until we're at the other end.

137
00:10:08,660 --> 00:10:09,360
Hey!

138
00:10:10,080 --> 00:10:11,080
F***ing damn it!

139
00:10:11,081 --> 00:10:15,180
Hey! Move that f***ing beam back! Move it back!

140
00:10:16,000 --> 00:10:20,440
My dad gets all huffy, you know, because this is really dangerous, you know, and I understand,

141
00:10:20,600 --> 00:10:24,420
but sometimes it gets taken out on me because I'm the youngest, you know.

142
00:10:27,340 --> 00:10:28,320
Lower it down.

143
00:10:28,820 --> 00:10:33,820
The main beams are inserted below the cross beams, providing support for the entire load.

144
00:10:41,080 --> 00:10:42,160
Good job, James.

145
00:10:43,700 --> 00:10:47,440
The main beams are chained together to keep them from spreading apart.

146
00:10:47,860 --> 00:10:50,500
If the beams and everything's placed straight, my wheels will be straight,

147
00:10:50,820 --> 00:10:52,280
and I'll be able to go straight down the road.

148
00:10:52,640 --> 00:10:56,300
If I put my beams crooked, then I'll be going crooked down the road.

149
00:10:56,960 --> 00:10:59,560
Let's get them jacks in. I want this on the wheels in an hour.

150
00:11:00,120 --> 00:11:03,560
Well, we got to get up high enough to look to the truck and put the wheels under it, whatever that is.

151
00:11:04,280 --> 00:11:04,980
A couple feet.

152
00:11:04,981 --> 00:11:11,900
Keep your safety down.

153
00:11:11,901 --> 00:11:15,900
Keep your safety down.

154
00:11:15,901 --> 00:11:20,900
Okay, here goes the back.

155
00:11:20,901 --> 00:11:23,900
Without warning, the barn tilts to one side.

156
00:11:23,901 --> 00:11:26,900
Hold, hold, hold, hold, hold.

157
00:11:26,901 --> 00:11:29,900
Why'd that tip like that?

158
00:11:29,901 --> 00:11:32,900
Was one end higher than the other back here?

159
00:11:32,901 --> 00:11:34,820
It just slid on the top of the jacks.

160
00:11:34,821 --> 00:11:37,660
OK, but was one end higher than the other?

161
00:11:37,661 --> 00:11:38,900
I thought it was a tiny high.

162
00:11:38,901 --> 00:11:40,520
I think you're higher over on that end,

163
00:11:40,521 --> 00:11:41,780
and that's why it came at us.

164
00:11:41,781 --> 00:11:44,840
We need to keep everything real level here, OK?

165
00:11:44,841 --> 00:11:46,400
Yeah, it was pretty scary.

166
00:11:46,401 --> 00:11:48,380
I was putting that crib in, and right then

167
00:11:48,381 --> 00:11:50,960
is when it flipped over towards me.

168
00:11:50,961 --> 00:11:52,580
You just got to always expect something.

169
00:11:56,780 --> 00:11:59,120
Once the barn is high enough, a crossbar

170
00:11:59,121 --> 00:12:02,140
is secured to the main beams in order to attach the truck.

171
00:12:02,141 --> 00:12:03,300
Okay, set it down.

172
00:12:14,120 --> 00:12:14,820
Come on.

173
00:12:15,820 --> 00:12:16,520
Go!

174
00:12:20,420 --> 00:12:21,360
We're in the hole.

175
00:12:24,880 --> 00:12:26,040
All right, set it down.

176
00:12:32,140 --> 00:12:39,640
Outside, James secures the front of the building with seven-strand quarter-inch tether cable.

177
00:12:39,820 --> 00:12:44,260
The building, as it comes downhill, this whole building is the one to fall over.

178
00:12:44,540 --> 00:12:49,020
That's 60, maybe 70,000 pounds leaning.

179
00:12:49,420 --> 00:12:54,060
We're going to secure this whole thing and the big giant X's, and I think we can hold her up.

180
00:12:56,240 --> 00:12:59,980
Yeah, well, we've got some rain, we've got some precipitation, the grass is wet.

181
00:12:59,981 --> 00:13:02,240
I think we better do this thing first thing in the morning.

182
00:13:03,020 --> 00:13:06,580
Bill calls it quits for the day, but the barn is ready to move.

183
00:13:09,480 --> 00:13:10,920
It's later that night.

184
00:13:11,280 --> 00:13:14,540
Norm Meyer Jr. has lived in the cabin for 30 years

185
00:13:14,541 --> 00:13:17,600
and has experienced several disturbing noises.

186
00:13:17,601 --> 00:13:23,240
It sounded like a baseball bat hitting a railroad rail, something like that.

187
00:13:23,560 --> 00:13:29,000
He has called in a paranormal investigator to rid the homestead of unwanted entities

188
00:13:29,001 --> 00:13:32,480
he feels were brought on by the four unexplained deaths.

189
00:13:33,840 --> 00:13:34,540
Hello?

190
00:13:35,700 --> 00:13:36,980
Is anyone here?

191
00:13:38,040 --> 00:13:41,460
The buildings here are going to leave.

192
00:13:41,640 --> 00:13:42,640
The house is leaving.

193
00:13:43,080 --> 00:13:44,140
The barn is leaving.

194
00:13:44,720 --> 00:13:45,820
You can't go with them.

195
00:13:46,420 --> 00:13:48,320
You can't follow the people who move it.

196
00:13:52,720 --> 00:13:54,720
It's day four in Colorado.

197
00:13:54,721 --> 00:13:59,720
An incoming snow front has turned this move into a race against time.

198
00:14:01,720 --> 00:14:06,720
100 years of haunted history begins its perilous journey.

199
00:14:17,720 --> 00:14:21,720
Average speed is measured not in miles, but in mere feet per hour.

200
00:14:24,720 --> 00:14:32,820
Sounds a little creaky, but what do you expect for 100 years old?

201
00:14:35,520 --> 00:14:36,620
It's all good.

202
00:14:38,540 --> 00:14:40,620
Bill approaches the steep hill.

203
00:14:41,080 --> 00:14:42,980
It could prove to be a barn killer.

204
00:14:44,780 --> 00:14:51,920
This descent is so steep, the two-yard loader is attached to act as a brake and prevent the barn from falling forward.

205
00:14:52,320 --> 00:14:54,060
You saw the speed I was going, right?

206
00:14:54,220 --> 00:14:54,920
Yeah.

207
00:14:54,921 --> 00:14:57,060
Okay, and that's the speed we're going to maintain down this hill.

208
00:14:57,380 --> 00:15:00,560
It's just in low gear, letting the tranny work.

209
00:15:00,920 --> 00:15:02,500
I got a little bit of brake going.

210
00:15:02,860 --> 00:15:04,500
You just keep tension on the chain.

211
00:15:12,880 --> 00:15:14,740
We're dragging a little bit on this beam.

212
00:15:21,880 --> 00:15:24,000
It's the sound of something wrong.

213
00:15:24,380 --> 00:15:25,700
I don't like the way it's leaning.

214
00:15:30,900 --> 00:15:34,680
45 tons of shifting weight has proven to be too much,

215
00:15:34,920 --> 00:15:37,120
snapping one of the internal tension cables.

216
00:15:37,640 --> 00:15:39,560
The barn has tilted to one side.

217
00:15:40,020 --> 00:15:42,680
So this is fine. It's just the top racked over a little bit,

218
00:15:43,100 --> 00:15:44,380
coming through the trees, I believe.

219
00:15:45,200 --> 00:15:46,100
We're tightened there, James?

220
00:15:46,940 --> 00:15:49,200
Yeah, the whole building's leaning that way.

221
00:15:49,360 --> 00:15:50,060
Yeah.

222
00:15:50,280 --> 00:15:53,260
They spend an hour tightening the remaining cables

223
00:15:53,261 --> 00:15:56,120
and re-welding the cross beams to the main beams.

224
00:15:57,280 --> 00:15:59,360
All right. Yeah, leave it like that.

225
00:15:59,840 --> 00:16:00,540
All right.

226
00:16:00,820 --> 00:16:05,040
Meanwhile, Bill wants to double-check a dangerous situation up ahead

227
00:16:05,041 --> 00:16:06,720
with these high-voltage wires.

228
00:16:06,980 --> 00:16:09,040
My building's at 27.6.

229
00:16:09,180 --> 00:16:10,760
I need a little bit of clearance.

230
00:16:11,220 --> 00:16:12,320
We've got a metal roof.

231
00:16:12,620 --> 00:16:14,280
We're hotter than a pistol up there,

232
00:16:14,281 --> 00:16:17,680
so we just got to make sure we don't have any incident.

233
00:16:18,860 --> 00:16:21,140
So, here's my lowest wire.

234
00:16:21,141 --> 00:16:28,140
We just broke 28 feet.

235
00:16:30,140 --> 00:16:33,140
Just about 29 feet. Looks like a green light. We're gonna go.

236
00:16:51,140 --> 00:17:02,340
Now they must make a tight 90 degree turn onto a very narrow road.

237
00:17:05,940 --> 00:17:08,820
The truck loses traction and they're stuck.

238
00:17:09,260 --> 00:17:12,220
The crew must attach the loader to hold them through the turn.

239
00:17:13,300 --> 00:17:15,740
Okay, you have it. Now nice and easy.

240
00:17:16,240 --> 00:17:17,120
Alright, let's go.

241
00:17:18,220 --> 00:17:18,920
Come on, Jason.

242
00:17:18,921 --> 00:17:25,620
