Good, right there.

488
00:33:01,641 --> 00:33:03,640
Next, the crew places the dollies in position.

489
00:33:03,641 --> 00:33:09,640
This is kind of a crucial point because we don't want to have to move two feet and jack

490
00:33:09,641 --> 00:33:10,640
up to reset a dolly.

491
00:33:10,641 --> 00:33:14,640
If one of these dollies get it out of kilter, it'll want to roll up or roll out from underneath

492
00:33:14,641 --> 00:33:15,700
just like a big roller skate.

493
00:33:15,800 --> 00:33:17,380
Yeah, we're back too far with this one.

494
00:33:17,900 --> 00:33:20,580
Placed up underneath the bulk of the house's weight,

495
00:33:20,920 --> 00:33:24,900
the two front dollies are state-of-the-art hydraulic power dollies

496
00:33:24,901 --> 00:33:26,820
propelled by a central pump.

497
00:33:27,120 --> 00:33:29,860
This is our propulsion unit.

498
00:33:30,460 --> 00:33:33,060
This is what's going to provide our mojo to take it down the road.

499
00:33:33,061 --> 00:33:35,220
When we're ready to move, we'll hook the hoses up to it,

500
00:33:35,660 --> 00:33:37,880
throw that joystick, and away we go.

501
00:33:39,660 --> 00:33:42,580
Finally, the skates are put under the main beams.

502
00:33:42,581 --> 00:33:46,700
With everything in place, the pressure in the jacks is released.

503
00:33:47,400 --> 00:33:50,500
180 tons comes to rest on the skates.

504
00:33:51,320 --> 00:33:52,020
What's that?

505
00:33:53,740 --> 00:33:57,040
It's a sound the crew never gets used to.

506
00:34:02,320 --> 00:34:05,780
When you've got that kind of weight coming down, you end up setting it on wood piles.

507
00:34:05,781 --> 00:34:08,880
No matter how rigid our steel is, you're still going to get some popping and cracking.

508
00:34:08,881 --> 00:34:14,740
The next day begins with a momentous and dangerous task.

509
00:34:15,220 --> 00:34:22,160
They will attempt to slide this 360,000-pound historic building 60 feet onto the dollies.

510
00:34:26,700 --> 00:34:28,240
Hold up, guys. I got to do something.

511
00:34:28,660 --> 00:34:29,360
Hold up a minute.

512
00:34:29,361 --> 00:34:30,320
Hold up a second, guys.

513
00:34:30,780 --> 00:34:32,520
One of the skates is off center.

514
00:34:33,800 --> 00:34:35,220
Everybody's drifting over a little bit.

515
00:34:36,720 --> 00:34:38,380
You see this roller's drifting off.

516
00:34:38,381 --> 00:34:39,823
We haven't got her pulled quite right.

517
00:34:39,824 --> 00:34:41,760
I think this end's coming just a little bit faster.

518
00:34:41,940 --> 00:34:44,220
We just don't want to get off the side of those rails too awful much.

519
00:34:44,820 --> 00:34:50,860
Ken uses the hydraulic jack within the power dollies to lift the house off the skate and get it re-centered.

520
00:35:03,180 --> 00:35:03,880
Okay.

521
00:35:04,760 --> 00:35:06,960
We can't be perfectionists all the time.

522
00:35:08,380 --> 00:35:17,320
The house completes the first step of its journey.

523
00:35:18,640 --> 00:35:22,860
As the final task for the day, the dollies are clamped onto the beams.

524
00:35:23,400 --> 00:35:26,380
Now it's all sitting on rubber-tired dollies and hydraulics.

525
00:35:26,700 --> 00:35:27,820
We're ready to take it down the road.

526
00:35:28,680 --> 00:35:30,400
Next on Mega Movers.

527
00:35:31,340 --> 00:35:36,180
Will the 180-ton house survive the perilous journey to its new home?

528
00:35:38,380 --> 00:35:46,560
It's day five, two days before the big move.

529
00:35:46,561 --> 00:35:51,380
The house needs to be rotated 180 degrees to point it on the designated route.

530
00:35:51,381 --> 00:35:54,500
We'll just take our time and ease it on out here.

531
00:35:54,501 --> 00:35:55,500
Let's give her a whirl.

532
00:35:55,501 --> 00:35:57,500
Let's make it happen.

533
00:35:57,501 --> 00:36:09,100
The guys are anxious to make it roll.

534
00:36:09,540 --> 00:36:13,820
The crew uses devices called come-alongs to steer the dollies.

535
00:36:14,580 --> 00:36:20,400
One end of a chain is attached to a fixed point on the beam, the other to the tongue of the dolly.

536
00:36:20,840 --> 00:36:26,240
A central ratchet is used to shorten the chain, pulling on the tongue and turning the dolly.

537
00:36:26,241 --> 00:36:30,180
I got my dad out front. He likes to be out front and calling some of the shots.

538
00:36:30,760 --> 00:36:32,540
And he's good at it. He knows what to look for.

539
00:36:32,740 --> 00:36:35,300
All I got to do is raise my finger and whirl it a little bit,

540
00:36:35,380 --> 00:36:37,740
and I think that's just about all this old man can handle.

541
00:36:45,120 --> 00:36:47,040
With the house fully turned around,

542
00:36:47,041 --> 00:36:50,780
the next task is to prepare the arrival site five blocks away.

543
00:36:51,300 --> 00:36:53,020
The concrete footings have been poured,

544
00:36:53,021 --> 00:36:59,200
and now wood cribbing is stacked to create two ramps that will need to support the 180-ton structure.

545
00:37:00,020 --> 00:37:03,120
Later, a brick foundation will be built up underneath it.

546
00:37:05,720 --> 00:37:07,320
These crib piles are real important.

547
00:37:07,520 --> 00:37:12,080
It doesn't look like much now, but when you get that great big house sitting over top of them, it'll mean a lot.

548
00:37:12,480 --> 00:37:14,620
One thing I'm nervous about, I wish Monday was tomorrow.

549
00:37:15,020 --> 00:37:17,120
Get it over with, maybe. Then I'll be happy.

550
00:37:18,640 --> 00:37:22,740
Monday morning, move day, and the town is ready for a show.

551
00:37:23,020 --> 00:37:24,507
I think it is fabulous.

552
00:37:24,508 --> 00:37:29,420
I think that this is the greatest thing that's ever happened in my lifetime.

553
00:37:29,720 --> 00:37:33,120
Well, that's the biggest thing that happened in Bloomington for a long time.

554
00:37:34,380 --> 00:37:36,420
That's incredible, really.

555
00:37:37,180 --> 00:37:46,500
Moving a house of this magnitude requires the combined coordination of the power, telephone, cable companies, public parks, and the local police.

556
00:37:47,140 --> 00:37:50,320
There's some excitement, and people love to see it. It's very unique.

557
00:37:50,321 --> 00:37:53,480
and we want to make sure the vehicles and people are safely at a distance.

558
00:37:55,020 --> 00:38:00,760
Today, the team will include several extra hands, including Ken's 11-year-old son, Luke.

559
00:38:01,020 --> 00:38:04,620
Hey, I'm tickled to death. That's the third generation or fourth generation now.

560
00:38:06,160 --> 00:38:09,140
I want to thank everybody for coming and giving me a hand on this move here today.

561
00:38:09,580 --> 00:38:16,860
We're going to try and refrain from yelling, so if there's a question, just go stop, get me, and we'll go take a look at it.

562
00:38:17,400 --> 00:38:19,160
Let's get it going and make her mobile.

563
00:38:20,320 --> 00:38:33,600
The house approaches its first difficult maneuver, a tight 90-degree turn.

564
00:38:34,020 --> 00:38:38,600
To see that come right at you practically, it's exciting.

565
00:38:39,520 --> 00:38:43,700
Cribbing is set down to ease the grade, but the crew cannot proceed.

566
00:38:44,580 --> 00:38:46,520
Hey, guys, I need you over here.

567
00:38:47,420 --> 00:38:49,280
The city foresters are called in.

568
00:38:49,281 --> 00:38:54,020
I'll basically wait for the house to get close, see what needs to be cleared.

569
00:38:55,020 --> 00:38:56,860
If it needs to be cleared, we'll cut it back.

570
00:38:57,240 --> 00:38:59,680
We just don't want to tear the roof clear off the house.

571
00:39:00,040 --> 00:39:01,120
Getting us a little clearance.

572
00:39:03,800 --> 00:39:08,180
Cautiously, the crew proceeds forward with only one objective.

573
00:39:09,000 --> 00:39:10,820
Turn. Turn, baby, turn.

574
00:39:12,140 --> 00:39:16,040
What we're trying to do is get the dolly up underneath the load, cranking on the come along.

575
00:39:16,041 --> 00:39:20,360
When you're setting up on all these dollies, it just takes a little more to steer them and work them around.

576
00:39:20,960 --> 00:39:23,940
And everybody laughs when you say, what do you need 14 guys for?

577
00:39:24,080 --> 00:39:25,700
I think every one of them is busy right now.

578
00:39:27,580 --> 00:39:33,440
Once the house hits the street, the crowd is treated to a parade unlike any they've ever seen.

579
00:39:46,040 --> 00:40:00,040
My son doing fine.

580
00:40:06,020 --> 00:40:08,180
He got it under control, had confidence.

581
00:40:08,720 --> 00:40:11,340
Beautiful, guys. We're making hay now.

582
00:40:12,260 --> 00:40:12,960
Progress.

583
00:40:12,961 --> 00:40:19,660
The power company is on hand to drop lines and remove light poles for a clear path.

584
00:40:19,661 --> 00:40:23,660
Move on down that way. Keep going that way. Out of the way.

585
00:40:23,661 --> 00:40:30,660
The house makes its final turn, only to face the greatest challenge of the day.

586
00:40:30,661 --> 00:40:36,660
The house must be guided along these two ramps of cribbing to sit over the foundation.

587
00:40:36,661 --> 00:40:38,660
All we've got to do is straight ahead.

588
00:40:38,661 --> 00:40:39,361
straight ahead.

589
00:40:39,362 --> 00:40:42,580
And now lineup becomes critical, so now it's just a matter of steering the

590
00:40:42,581 --> 00:40:46,660
dollies in, making sure our cribs are up. And this is where our eight hours in that

591
00:40:46,661 --> 00:40:48,660
hole the other day is really going to pay off.

592
00:40:48,661 --> 00:40:54,660
Does everybody want to keep it safe now? Safety block.

593
00:40:54,661 --> 00:40:59,273
It's a perilous final step, where every inch counts.

594
00:40:59,274 --> 00:41:02,200
The house could slip off the side

595
00:41:02,201 --> 00:41:06,340
of the cribbing, so everyone must be in absolute sync.

596
00:41:06,341 --> 00:41:08,580
In a sense, you trust these guys with your life.

597
00:41:09,060 --> 00:41:10,280
Everybody protects everybody else.

598
00:41:10,320 --> 00:41:12,040
Everybody watches everybody's back, I should say.

599
00:41:12,280 --> 00:41:14,660
Okay, looks like I need to maybe come this way a little.

600
00:41:15,040 --> 00:41:15,840
I'm a worrier.

601
00:41:16,500 --> 00:41:18,180
We're getting awful close right back there.

602
00:41:20,300 --> 00:41:22,720
This is a little bit different moving than my dad's used to.

603
00:41:22,840 --> 00:41:23,880
He gets a little uncomfortable.

604
00:41:24,220 --> 00:41:25,740
He's got his whole family underneath there,

605
00:41:25,760 --> 00:41:26,660
and if something would go wrong,

606
00:41:26,720 --> 00:41:28,000
he'd probably take some of us with it.

607
00:41:28,001 --> 00:41:37,300
chalk it up good enough

608
00:41:37,301 --> 00:41:45,700
let's go over here and have a drink

609
00:41:45,701 --> 00:41:48,840
when things go well they go well and they went well here

610
00:41:48,841 --> 00:41:51,620
I think everybody's kind of admired it and I do too

611
00:41:51,621 --> 00:41:55,820
it's important that we preserve our history

612
00:41:55,821 --> 00:41:59,020
We've got to leave something for our kids to look at.

613
00:41:59,440 --> 00:42:00,340
This is important.

614
00:42:00,820 --> 00:42:03,880
To house movers like Bill Davis and Ken Bolaña,

615
00:42:04,340 --> 00:42:08,380
the sense of accomplishment comes from not only saving a piece of history,

616
00:42:08,740 --> 00:42:10,720
but doing it alongside family.

617
00:42:11,360 --> 00:42:12,400
It means everything to me.

618
00:42:12,480 --> 00:42:14,820
The support I got empowers me to do what I do.

619
00:42:15,740 --> 00:42:17,240
Everything he done worked out.

620
00:42:17,980 --> 00:42:21,280
Find something that you'd like to do and be proud of it.

621
00:42:21,320 --> 00:42:22,780
We're proud of it, proud of our heritage,

622
00:42:22,980 --> 00:42:24,800
and my grandfather would be proud today.

623
00:42:24,801 --> 00:42:25,501
He would.

